SWARM 1b — STALE-FEED QUARANTINE for the accuracy registry
==========================================================

TARGET FILES  tools/accuracy_registry.py
              tools/test_accuracy_registry.py
BASE          branch hmm-regime-and-pbo, HEAD 0b1f73b, both files clean at HEAD

APPLY         from the repository root:
                  git apply --check round2-drafts/patches/grader-stale-feed.patch
                  git apply         round2-drafts/patches/grader-stale-feed.patch
REVERT            git apply -R      round2-drafts/patches/grader-stale-feed.patch

CONFLICTS WITH round2-drafts/patches/grader-quarantine.patch (swarm 1a). Both
edit the same three regions of accuracy_registry.py (the helper block before
connect(), the fetch_directional_days SQL, the fetch_calibration_bins SQL) and
the same JSON payload anchor, and 1a aliases the table as `po` while this one
would introduce the same alias itself. Apply ONE of the two, or apply
round2-drafts/patches/grader-quarantine-stale.patch, which carries both.

WHAT IT DOES
  Drops from every directional tally and every reliability bin any graded
  observation minted on a trading day the daemon had already flagged that
  SYMBOL's own feed stale — dq_events(kind='stale'), written per symbol per hour
  by internal/maintain/maintain.go's DQAuditor. The call was computed from
  inputs the daemon had itself declared unfit, so grading it measures the
  outage, not the predictor.

  Nothing is written and nothing is relabelled. Every row stays in
  prediction_outcomes exactly as frozen; only the tally changes.

WHY dq_events AND NOT srchealth
  internal/srchealth writes dq_events(kind='source_stale') — 52 rows, ALL with
  symbol_id NULL, because it grades SOURCES (tv_quotes, news, bars_1m_hot), not
  symbols. It names no symbol and therefore cannot key a per-prediction
  exclusion. kind='stale' is the only per-symbol staleness writer in the tree
  (maintain.go:570).

THE DELISTING FLOOD IS INERT — MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED
  Live DB, post-epoch: 7,486 kind='stale' events, of which 6,346 sit on the
  1,868 symbols carrying delisted_at. Those 1,868 symbols intersect the 336
  symbols in the graded population in ZERO symbols, so the (symbol, trading-day)
  join drops nothing on their account: 296 observations are excluded whether or
  not delisted tickers are carved out (both counts measured). No carve-out is
  added, deliberately — delisted_at is a property of the SYMBOL, not of a day,
  so carving it out would also stop excluding the days after a mid-window feed
  death, which are exactly the days that must go.

MEASURED EFFECT (live DB read-only and the 20260806-131007 backup, identical)
  graded observations   8,567 -> 8,271   (296 excluded, 3.5%, 44 symbols, 11 days)
  directional-ensemble (1d)   44.01% (n=3,322) -> 43.79% (n=3,227)   -0.22 pp
  directional-ensemble (1w)   41.06% (n=1,656) -> 40.67% (n=1,591)   -0.39 pp
  1d high conviction          48.63% (n=621)   -> 48.27% (n=607)     -0.36 pp
  prequential-majority (1d)   57.74%           -> 58.01%
  NO verdict changes. The 1d ensemble stays FAILED; every other row stays
  INSUFFICIENT DAYS.

HONESTY SURFACE
  measure_stale_feed_exclusion() is run on every DB grade and its result is
  printed beside universe completeness and carried in the JSON payload as
  stale_feed_exclusion. On a snapshot grade (no dq_events table) it publishes
  applied=false with a reason: an absent filter must never read as a clean feed.

PERFORMANCE
  The stale keys are folded once in a CTE. trading_day() is a Python callback,
  so the obvious correlated-subquery form calls it per (outcome row x dq row)
  pair: measured 245.21s on the live corpus, against 0.12s for the CTE form and
  0.10s unfiltered. All three forms return the identical 8,271-row population.

TESTS  python3 -m unittest discover -s tools -p 'test_*.py'
  119 pass (114 before + 5 added). Mutation-checked: disabling the clause while
  leaving the helper in place fails 3 of the 5 new tests.

PRECONDITION THIS PATCH DOES NOT SATISFY
  prereg_records seq 50 pins graderSha256
  b826f39775b972ab28115ca1ae2ab76be157213f880316871083d8f80dbd12e4, which is the
  byte-for-byte digest of tools/accuracy_registry.py at HEAD. ANY edit to that
  file — this one, 1a's, or both — re-hashes the grader off the pin, and
  require_registered_grader() then refuses to grade. A new grading-protocol
  chain record naming the new digest must be written before the registry can
  publish again. That write is a daemon/chain operation and is deliberately NOT
  part of this patch.

